---
<p class="lede">A function names an operation. Bayan puts the type first, and most calls label arguments with a short word such as <code>from:</code> or <code>by:</code>: a call reads like a phrase. This chapter goes from one-line functions to labelled, defaulted and <code>ref</code> parameters.</p>

<h2 id="basic">Declaring and calling</h2>
<example name="book-04-basic"/>
<ul>
  <li>A declaration is return type, name, parameters: <code>i32 square(i32 x)</code>; parameters are type first too.</li>
  <li><code>return</code> gives the result; <code>void</code> means nothing is returned.</li>
  <li>A one-expression body uses <code>=&gt;</code> instead of braces and <code>return</code>: <code>i32 cube(i32 x) =&gt; x * square(x)</code>.</li>
  <li>They can call themselves, as <code>factorial</code> does.</li>
  <li>Parameters are read-only; this is refused:</li>
</ul>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `n` is immutable
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> immutparam.byn:2:5

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     n -= 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>     ^ parameters are immutable
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: copy it into a local `var`, or take it by `ref` if the caller's value should change</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2104</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check immutparam.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>immutparam.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="particles">Particles: labelled arguments</h2>
<p>A function may have one unlabeled parameter, the thing it works on. Every other required parameter carries a particle, one of <code>from to until through at by for as</code>, before the type in the declaration and a colon label at the call.</p>
<example name="book-04-particles"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>transfer(200, from: "Aisha", to: "Ali")</code> reads as a sentence; <code>200</code> is the unlabeled argument.</li>
  <li>Labelled arguments come in any order: the second call puts <code>to:</code> before <code>from:</code>.</li>
  <li>Labels are fixed by the declaration: you cannot invent or drop one; dropping one is the most common error:</li>
</ul>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5005:</span> `payer` is passed without its particle `from:`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> particle.byn:5:15

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> transfer(200, "Aisha", "Ali")
<span class="gut">    │</span>               ^^^^^^^ needs `from:`
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: add `from:` before the argument</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 5 │</span> transfer(200, from: "Aisha", "Ali")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">               ++++++</span>

<span class="err">error BYN5005:</span> `payee` is passed without its particle `to:`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> particle.byn:5:24

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> transfer(200, "Aisha", "Ali")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                        ^^^^^ needs `to:`
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: add `to:` before the argument</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 5 │</span> transfer(200, "Aisha", to: "Ali")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">                        ++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN5005</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check particle.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>particle.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Each argument gets its own error with the exact edit; the suggestions come one at a time; apply both.</p>
<p>Particle-less parameters are reported at the declaration:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5004:</span> `announce` has 2 unlabeled parameters; give each but one a particle
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> twoplain.byn:1:6

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> void announce(string label, i32 value) {
<span class="gut">    │</span>      ^^^^^^^^ 2 unlabeled parameters
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: keep one unlabeled parameter, the object</span>

<span class="err">error BYN5006:</span> `value` is a required parameter besides the object and needs a particle
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> twoplain.byn:1:33

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> void announce(string label, i32 value) {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                 ^^^^^ needs a particle
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: label it with `from`, `to`, `until`, `through`, `at`, `by`, `for` or `as`</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN5004</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check twoplain.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>twoplain.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The first error says what is wrong, the second what each other parameter needs. Pick by role: <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> source and destination, <code>until</code>/<code>through</code> exclusive and inclusive ends, <code>at</code> position, <code>by</code> method or step, <code>for</code> subject, <code>as</code> representation.</p>

<h2 id="mistakes">Wrong arguments</h2>
<p>A wrong type:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i32`, found `string`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> argtype.byn:3:17

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> print($"{square("seven")}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 ^^^^^^^

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check argtype.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>argtype.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A wrong count:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2302:</span> `square` takes 1 argument(s)
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> argcount.byn:3:20

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> print($"{square(2, 3)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                    ^

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2302</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check argcount.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>argcount.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="defaults">Default values</h2>
<p>A defaulted parameter can be left out, or overridden by name.</p>
<example name="book-04-defaults"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>string greeting = "Salam"</code> is optional; <code>greet("Aisha")</code> uses the default.</li>
  <li>A defaulted parameter is passed by name: <code>greeting: "Welcome"</code>, <code>times: 5</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="ref"><code>ref</code> parameters</h2>
<p>By default a function gets a read-only view of its arguments; to change the caller's variable, mark the parameter <code>ref</code> and write it at the call.</p>
<example name="book-04-ref"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>bump(ref hits)</code> adds one to the caller's <code>hits</code>; the word at the call says <code>hits</code> may change.</li>
  <li>Pass a <code>var</code>, as <code>hits</code> is here.</li>
  <li>A <code>ref</code> parameter can come with defaulted parameters, as <code>addTwice(ref xs, value: 9)</code> shows.</li>
</ul>
<p>Forget it at the call and the compiler adds it:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2306:</span> argument for `counter` must be passed as `ref`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> noref.byn:6:6

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> bump(hits)
<span class="gut">    │</span>      ^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 6 │</span> bump(ref hits)
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">      ++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2306</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check noref.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>noref.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="rest">Any number of arguments: <code>rest</code></h2>
<p>The last parameter can be <code>rest</code>: any number of values as a read-only span.</p>
<example name="book-04-rest"/>
<p>Inside, <code>numbers</code> is a <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;i32&gt;</code>, walked with <code>for</code>; calling with no values is allowed.</p>

<h2 id="overloads">Overloads</h2>
<p>Functions can share a name when their particle sets differ:</p>
<example name="book-04-overloads"/>
<p>The compiler picks the one whose particles match; overloads differing only by an unlabeled parameter's type are not accepted:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2006:</span> `show` is declared twice
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> overload.byn:2:8

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> string show(string s) =&gt; $"text {s}"
<span class="gut">    │</span>        ^^^^ already declared on line 1

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2006</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check overload.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>overload.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The second is a duplicate: give the functions different names (<code>showNumber</code>, <code>showText</code>) or particles.</p>

<h2 id="discard">Ignoring a result</h2>
<p>An unneeded result can be bound to <code>_</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let _ = list.removeLast()</code></pre>
<p>Some calls, such as <code>removeLast()</code>, must have their result bound or used; otherwise the compiler reports <code>BYN3201</code>. Help text sometimes suggests <code>_ = f()</code>; that form does not work yet, so write <code>let _ = f()</code>.</p>

<h2 id="recap">What you have now</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Type first: <code>i32 square(i32 x)</code>; <code>=&gt;</code> for one-expression bodies.</li>
  <li>One unlabeled parameter; every other required parameter has a particle.</li>
  <li>Defaults are passed by name; <code>ref</code> is written at the declaration and at the call.</li>
  <li>Parameters are read-only unless <code>ref</code>.</li>
  <li>Overloads differ by particle set.</li>
</ul>
<p>The full rules are in <a href="parameters.html">Parameters and arguments</a>.</p>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<example name="book-04-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>[plain] a
[bold] b</code></pre>
  <p>The first call omits <code>as:</code>, so <code>style</code> defaults to <code>plain</code>; the second passes <code>as: "bold"</code>. <code>label("b", "bold")</code> would not compile: the argument needs its particle.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-04-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>1 11</code></pre>
  <p><code>var b = a</code> copies the number; <code>bump(ref b)</code> changes only <code>b</code>, so <code>a</code> stays 1 and <code>b</code> becomes 11. <code>11 11</code> assumes one shared value, which numbers are not.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>You write <code>void move(string name, i32 steps)</code>. What does the compiler say, and what is a fix?</summary>
  <p><code>BYN5004</code> and <code>BYN5006</code>: one unlabeled parameter only, and the second needs a particle. Write <code>void move(string name, by i32 steps)</code> and call <code>move("Aisha", by: 3)</code>; a default on <code>steps</code> also compiles, with the call <code>move("Aisha", steps: 3)</code>.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>A function body says <code>n -= 1</code> where <code>n</code> is a parameter. Why is it refused, and what are the two fixes?</summary>
  <p><code>BYN2104</code>: parameters are read-only. If only the function needs the value changed, copy it (<code>var m = n</code>); if the caller's variable should change, declare <code>ref i32 n</code> and call with <code>ref</code>. A <code>var</code> at the caller does not help: the rule is about the parameter.</p>
</details>

